WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.330
ROKESH JANKIE: So
let's get started.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.700
Hadoop on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.380
First of all, it's
extremely fast to set it up.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:18.230
So nowadays, if you go to
probably the IT department

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.770
within your company, you'll
end up with the question,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:24.440
oh, I need a Hadoop cluster
and it needs to be this big.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.340
And next up, you get a
planning of five weeks or more

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.300
just to get started.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.047
We need to
investigate, et cetera.

00:00:31.047 --> 00:00:32.630
So what I'm going
to show you later on

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.370
is that it can be done in
like one and a half minutes

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:40.070
that you have a cluster ready
for operations with your data.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:47.570
Another thing is, Hadoop is most
of the time seen as something

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:49.490
like a low-level
infrastructure thing that

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.120
is happening within the
whole cluster itself.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.900
But actually, it's quite hard
being a good data engineer,

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.280
but what we want is that
you focus on your data

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.620
and not necessarily on
the infrastructure level.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.380
That's something that
we can manage for you.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:08.000
And most importantly,
you don't pay upfront

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.550
like thousands of dollars.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.510
You just start.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.510
And you'll see that
scaling up, scaling

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:16.670
down is something
that's very flexible

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:22.190
and actually built in
in the data product.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:27.260
So if you look at the landscape
or the whole scope of where

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.160
we are today, you can go from
infrastructure to platform.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.050
What does that mean?

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.550
On the one side,
you see on premise,

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.200
and that's actually how many
organizations are doing it.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.480
They're trying it out.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.290
And yeah, so at some moment,
you have an [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.820
for Hadoop like Cloudera
or another party which can

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.310
help you in this whole process.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.920
There are some tools, like
bdutil which actually allows

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.510
you to do command
line activities

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.809
and create a cluster, but if
you want to have it like manage

00:01:57.809 --> 00:01:59.600
but still have control
on everything what's

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.480
happening in there, Cloud
Data Park is your best friend.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.300
And actually, the
last three categories

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.610
are provided by the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.780
So let's take a peek under the
hood of what Google Cloud Data

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.570
Park is.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.400
First of all, you have
to think in layers.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.460
The layer, which is actually
quite important, is--

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.820
first of all, think of who's
interacting with the system.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.290
Then, you have the system.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.810
And then, you have the
ecosystem of all the things

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.569
that are connected to
your Hadoop cluster.

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:35.360
I'd like to think,
also, in Hadoop cluster,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.640
because it's nothing
else than that.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.030
So if you're used to working
with your other cluster,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.560
yes, you can.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.930
If you want to do an
SSH to you other cluster

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.577
and run some Hadoop
jobs, yes, you can.

00:02:48.577 --> 00:02:49.910
But you have to think in layers.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.880
And the good part
is, you have options.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.555
You can always go
like you wanted to go,

00:02:55.555 --> 00:02:57.710
or like you did it so far.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.380
We also provide some
APIs, which actually

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.630
have something nifty in there.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:05.630
Because yes, I can start
a job, close my laptop,

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.820
go home, and see
the job finished.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.580
So that's something that's
quite different with Hadoop jobs

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.490
that you probably have
in your cluster now.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:17.360
Another thing is
that the Hadoop stack

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.660
that we have is of Apache beta.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:25.090
That's actually like a
collection of Spark, Hadoop,

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.110
Hive, Pig, those tools that
are familiar in the Hadoop

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.070
stack itself.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.060
On the other side,
of course, you

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.050
have the data processing part.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.630
Then, you the analytics part.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.180
And on the analytics
part, you see

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.510
that people plug in lots
of third-party tools

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:46.280
like Jupyter, Presto,
Zeppelin to that cluster.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.910
And that's actually how we like
to see that you start using it.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:55.800
So this is how Cloud
Dataproc is in the end.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.220
So if you're planning
a PoC, a P-O-C,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.980
there's one thing
that's happening,

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:04.370
I think-- and maybe the people
who just raised their hands

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:07.340
can confirm it-- that you start
off with a Hadoop cluster,

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:08.830
you say, OK, I have it.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.630
Then you say, OK, now we
need something special--

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.999
Spark, PySpark, Hive, Pig,
everything on top of it.

00:04:15.999 --> 00:04:17.790
Then, all of a sudden,
your data scientists

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.269
say, OK, I'd like
to access that data.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:22.980
And then the plug in
Jupyter Notebook, et cetera.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.530
So the good part
of the Hadoop stack

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.770
itself is that you can augment
that whole environment quite

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:28.380
well.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.920
It's built for integration.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.980
But on the other side, it
becomes a monolith in years.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:37.300
And all of a sudden, people
say, OK, you know, it's running.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.720
I'm not going to touch it.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.940
And that's exactly why you have
to think not about lifting it

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.325
to a new environment,
but maybe you

00:04:46.325 --> 00:04:48.170
have to rethink
what you're using.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.120
And maybe you should
start to figure out,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.640
OK, this is a
workaround, and this

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.730
is something I really need.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.710
And that's something we're
going to see in a bit also.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:03.620
So this is what you see,
quite often, happening,

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.950
which is actually
another good thing.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:11.930
So if you look at the goals
that people can have, it's--

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.060
most of the time, it's
about cost savings--

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:17.380
how much do I save with it?

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.720
Our advice is, don't
go byte by byte,

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.210
but look at the whole
experience of it,

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.490
price, performance
in that context.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:28.240
Scaling elasticity-- so
multi-cluster scale-out

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.910
is very easy.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.790
If you want a burst for
just a couple of minutes,

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.810
it's actually quite easy.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.520
If you don't have that capacity,
and you need it at that moment,

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.920
you can just say
it to the system.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.440
And then, it will
give it to you back.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.760
I'm saying say it.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:49.420
Unfortunately, I don't have a
Hadoop demo with Google Home

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:50.140
device.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:54.280
That would be really cool,
but that's for next time.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.467
Then, for
development-- actually,

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:59.050
if you look at this
list, you probably

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:00.758
find one of the reasons
that you say, OK,

00:06:00.758 --> 00:06:03.250
that's the reason why I
would like to move forward

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:08.480
to something new, because it's
becoming really expensive.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.019
So another thing
that is something

00:06:11.019 --> 00:06:13.060
that you should start
figuring out for yourself--

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.018
because each of the
environments are different,

00:06:15.018 --> 00:06:16.390
they have a different need.

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.070
And that's actually
also how it starts.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.580
Because it's not about,
I have one cluster,

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.870
and I'm going to do
everything with it.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.640
Most of the time, that is
the end result of what's

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.152
happening now with
Hadoop in production.

00:06:30.152 --> 00:06:32.110
So what you have to figure
out for yourself is,

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:33.630
what open-source
components are I

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:36.370
using, what is,
indeed, necessary,

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:39.035
and what is a workaround.

00:06:39.035 --> 00:06:41.140
You might find some
things that you say, OK, I

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:42.970
shouldn't have done it.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:45.490
But now I have a new option
to start doing it again,

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:47.240
but differently.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.460
Another thing is the
system characteristics.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:53.860
So we can natively
support HBase,

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:58.945
which is also very common
in a Hadoop cluster, HDFS,

00:06:58.945 --> 00:07:03.010
of course, and many other
protocols that are quite known

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.680
in the Google Cloud.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.330
But again, you
have to figure out,

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:09.550
yourself, what is necessary
for your environment.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:10.440
Is it about SSDs?

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.250
Is it about, you
know, hard disks?

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:13.680
Is it about cold storage?

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.850
Is it data that I access
a lot, yes or no--

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:21.910
many characteristics that are
typical to your environment--

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:24.580
and also about the timeline,
your quota, your capacity.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:29.310
So you know, it's very
hard to say, now, today--

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:31.540
I have encountered
one such situation

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:34.345
that people had to
bring in, seriously,

00:07:34.345 --> 00:07:39.610
a sever rack,
configure it, and try

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:43.930
to do some calculations on
it, or some data science.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:46.824
But in the end, that's
not where the value is.

00:07:46.824 --> 00:07:47.740
It's not the hardware.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:48.950
It's not the software also.

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:50.890
It's about what you get
out of your data that

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:55.510
is so common, so particular
to this specific client.

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:57.970
So that's why I would
say another thing that's

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:02.770
very important in this-- how do
I get my data into the system?

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.270
All this runs
around data, right?

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.557
And we're collecting
quite a lot nowadays.

00:08:06.557 --> 00:08:08.140
So if you look at
the storage options,

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.340
you have a couple of them--

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:13.720
the Google Cloud Storage,
which is an object store--

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:19.710
so it has some other semantics--

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.090
HDFS and Dataproc
persistent disk--

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:24.430
so if you want to
have performance.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:26.540
But if you want to go
higher in that context,

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:29.570
you can opt for SSDs.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.280
Another thing is, if you
want to say, OK, fine,

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.650
I don't want to
manage my data at all,

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:38.169
then something like
Bigtable, Cloud Bigtable,

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:41.414
comes in, which, in fact,
is an HBase implementation.

00:08:41.414 --> 00:08:42.789
So everything you
know from HBase

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:44.910
can be reused in
this context also.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.890
And if you really don't want to
worry about the underlying part

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:52.620
and want to use, as mentioned
also in the keynote, Bigquery,

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.830
which is a no-ops data warehouse
solution that Google provides

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.470
in the cloud where you can
do petabyte-scale queries

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:01.990
in, like, minutes--

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.820
and it's becoming,
really, more important,

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.580
because we have some customers
that are generating petabytes

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:08.680
of data a day.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:10.840
So then, Bigquery might
be another solution

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.100
to look at for storage.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:18.310
Of course, if you look
at the timeline, it's--

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.800
some people use the
storage for archiving

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.780
or for backup recovery.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:26.560
The nice part is, if you try
to put it in these storage

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:32.440
options, you have an option to
run ad-hoc queries if you want,

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.624
because you have that
environment ready.

00:09:34.624 --> 00:09:36.040
You don't have to
replicate a lot.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:39.100
So that might be another
thing to think about.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:43.510
OK, if you look at--

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:47.560
so the Google
Cloud Connector is,

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:52.000
in fact, one of the cornerstones
of the whole implementation

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.720
if you're going to
go Google Cloud.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:54.220
Why?

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:57.145
Because first of all, you
have to get your data in.

00:09:57.145 --> 00:09:59.020
And I'm going to show
you a couple of options

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:02.338
that you have to get your
data into the system.

00:10:02.338 --> 00:10:08.345
But first of all,
GSF, GCS, and HDFS

00:10:08.345 --> 00:10:12.880
are implementations
that are interoperable.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:19.030
You can quite easily implement
the access to your files,

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:23.330
actually, by changing the
protocols to, for example,

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:23.830
GS://.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:29.530
There is a difference though.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:32.050
HDFS has a different semantics.

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:34.240
And I mentioned, a moment
ago, with semantics,

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:36.200
I mean to say that we
have something called--

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:39.920
in HDFS, we have something
called directories.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.010
Cloud Storage doesn't
have that context.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:46.000
The nice part is that
the world of Hadoop

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.600
is starting to understand that
whole concept of not having

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:50.680
directories.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:54.400
Because if you look at it
from a different angle,

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.050
it might not make sense anymore.

00:10:56.050 --> 00:11:00.590
Because we're doing unstructured
data all over the place.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:02.290
There is a pro
and a con on that,

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:06.190
of course, because the fact
that we don't have directories

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:09.460
and other things that
are different than HDFS

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:13.060
has to do with the fact that we
want to have high availability.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.360
So it's up to you.

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:20.710
There are ways to
still do it anyhow.

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:27.950
But for example, if you have
files on which data is added--

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:29.450
so they are appended.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:31.150
They append data to it--

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:35.300
then you might have to
look into the native HDFS

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:39.820
implementation on Google Cloud
Storage-- of Google Cloud.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.940
So in the end, there
are pros and cons.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.070
But the most important
part is that you do

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.410
realize that you have options.

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:49.990
You have options to
move forward also.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:53.680
So getting data in your system
can be done in two ways.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:58.570
You have the push-based version
versus the pull-based version.

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:00.430
What does it mean?

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:03.220
So there's tooling on
the command line which

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:07.330
you can, say, distribute a
copy, and all my files are

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:09.430
pumped into the Cloud Storage.

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:12.200
That's just a command
line tool that you can do.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.620
Then you use your internet
gateway, and that's it.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:18.140
That's the easiest
way to go forward.

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:21.050
But you can, of course, imagine
that a customer would say,

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:23.885
well, no, that's too leaky,
although it's over HTTPS,

00:12:23.885 --> 00:12:27.850
et cetera, I still want to
have-- with my firewall rules,

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:31.930
and with my routing information,
I want to have access--

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:35.440
sorry, I want to provide my
data to the cloud environment.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:40.810
So for that, Google provides
Google Cloud VPN gateway,

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:43.930
which allows you to create
a private subnetwork

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:47.770
within the Google Cloud
platform which fits your network

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:49.750
needs for your firewall
and actually creates

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.300
a bridge between the
cloud environment

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:53.860
and your environment.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:56.230
The most interesting part
is that you can query

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:57.400
from both environments now.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.800
You can start querying
from the cloud environment,

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.450
because it's like a bridge.

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:03.950
You can query on the
on-premise situation,

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.190
depending on the firewall rules.

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:07.850
But also, from the
other side, you just

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:10.054
can query the data that's
stored in Google Cloud.

00:13:10.054 --> 00:13:11.837
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:11.837 --> 00:13:13.170
ROKESH JANKIE: Does makes sense.

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:20.210
So there are a couple of
options that you have here.

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:23.950
So another result of
doing all these migrations

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:25.330
and thinking forward--

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:28.710
and of course, if you have
pain, you try to fix it--

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:33.460
is to think about some bigger
architectural concepts.

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:39.150
So one of the most important
things in Cloud Dataproc is--

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:44.430
the way we think about it is,
if people start a cluster now,

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:47.370
the first question that
you get is, how much

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:48.660
data are they going to store.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:52.200
So people start ramping
up hard disks and saying,

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:57.680
OK, I want so much petabytes of
data for a hard disk available.

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:00.390
We, in Google Cloud, think
differently on this context.

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.810
Because this concept
in Hadoop, which

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:08.030
is called bin packing
and data locality,

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:12.780
both things are
very important when

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:17.280
you start to implement your
Hadoop cluster on premise

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.340
or in a hosted environment.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:23.910
But the most important part is
that if you start doing that,

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:25.740
you're adding complexity
to your cluster.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:27.785
Because managing that part is--

00:14:27.785 --> 00:14:29.160
what I mentioned
in the beginning

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:32.520
is it's going to create
[INAUDIBLE] in your operations.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.596
And it's going to
make it more complex.

00:14:34.596 --> 00:14:35.970
The cool part of
this whole story

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:40.550
is that the Hadoop cluster,
with separate storage,

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:44.700
supports bin packing
out of the box.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.950
And data locality is
something you don't even

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:48.000
have to think about.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.160
Because the Google
Cloud can make sure

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.170
that the data is
close to you depending

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:55.230
on how you start your cluster.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.930
So if you do this,
that's another note

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.440
that is very important.

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:04.650
So if you define
compute and storage--

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.419
if you define those two--

00:15:06.419 --> 00:15:08.460
you can start thinking in
different concepts now.

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:11.780
Because if I say--

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:15.240
if, initially, you said that the
computation power of-- sorry,

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:17.550
the computation hours--

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.290
were like 10,000--

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:23.630
10,000 hours to, for example,
do a data analysis part--

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:29.880
you now can say, OK, so that was
one hour, 10,000 CPUs, vCPUs.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:33.930
And now you can say, OK,
give me 20,000 vCPUs.

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:37.020
And in a half an hour, I
have my data processed.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:41.140
Or give me 100 vCPUs and
100 hours of processing.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:42.390
That's up to you.

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.730
The good part is you have
the freedom in the end.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:47.910
Because in the end, the total
cost of all those three options

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:49.120
are the same.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:52.470
If you do 10,000 one
hour, 20,000 half an hour,

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:55.916
or for 100 in 100 hours, the
cost for the Google Cloud

00:15:55.916 --> 00:15:56.790
platform is the same.

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:59.430
So now you see that
you can process

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:02.160
the same kind of information
in less time, because you

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:05.910
can burst and allocate
quite some CPUs.

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:08.190
Because you've make the
distinction between storage

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.380
and compute.

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:15.750
Another very nice thing-- and I
noticed it in my demo also when

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:16.950
I was preparing this--

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.340
is the cost saving
through preemptible VMs.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.080
What are preemptible VMs?

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.770
So who of you are
in the dev ops area,

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:28.290
who are building and
creating VMs, maybe, in your

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:29.630
build process?

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:32.081
So preemptible VMs
are exactly meant

00:16:32.081 --> 00:16:33.330
for those kinds of situations.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:38.010
Because what happens is that,
if you build, you run a test,

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:39.660
and then you kill
the VM again, right?

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:48.390
Normally, you would buy a
contract, or sign for a year,

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:52.190
or have an hourly
billing situation,

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:54.730
and even if you use
it, like, 10 minutes.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:59.550
So a preemptible VM-- so first
of all, minute-based billing.

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:02.160
And another thing
is they are like 20%

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:03.690
of the price of an original VM.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:06.880
Because we know you're
going to throw it away.

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:08.339
And that's another thing.

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:11.339
If you don't throw
it away, Google

00:17:11.339 --> 00:17:14.865
has the right to
reclaim that capacity.

00:17:14.865 --> 00:17:16.740
Because if you're not
doing anything with it,

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:20.790
and it's not meant to live
long in your cluster--

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:24.869
and that's actually the nicest
part of Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:26.720
As fast as you
bring it up-- like,

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:28.079
in one and a half minutes--

00:17:28.079 --> 00:17:30.629
even faster, you
can break it down.

00:17:30.629 --> 00:17:31.920
And that's actually quite cool.

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:33.210
And it saves quite some money.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.340
Because each of the VMs
that we have is, like,

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.280
20% of the cost
of an original VM.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:42.250
And I calculated, the simple
VM is, like, $1 a day.

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:47.940
And it's $0.20-- sorry, it's
$1 per hour, more or less.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:53.760
And it'll become,
like, $0.20 per hour.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.970
Another thing that the
Google Cloud supports

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:01.230
is that if you have a workload
like on that side, which

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:04.380
is full, you might
notice that you are not

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:06.034
using the full capacity.

00:18:06.034 --> 00:18:07.200
And that's the thing, right?

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.530
We have a shortage of DRAM,
and hardware, and stuff.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:14.820
And it's going to
cost you if you

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:18.480
start using quite large
clusters which are incidentally

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:19.100
using it.

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:20.850
That's going to cost
you quite some money.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:22.500
So what you can do
here, and that's

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.440
actually a quite nice
feature of Google Cloud--

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.570
we have rightsizing in place.

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:31.666
So you can even say, I want
three CPUs, instead of four,

00:18:31.666 --> 00:18:36.570
with a strange
amount of gigabytes.

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:40.330
The craziest configuration
is possible in this context.

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:45.559
Another thing is that if
you use the Google Cloud,

00:18:45.559 --> 00:18:47.850
you will also notice that we
are going to recommend you

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:49.933
at some moment, because
we noticed that you're not

00:18:49.933 --> 00:18:51.100
using the full capacity.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:53.070
And we like to
advise you on that.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:55.380
Because you're going to get
a cost-saving marker also,

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:56.430
in your console.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.860
And another thing is,
don't make just one

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:04.170
cluster to do everything.

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:05.880
Think about your clusters.

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:09.450
Because you separate the
compute power from storage,

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:12.240
you can now think about doing
the ETL stuff in a different

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:13.960
cluster than your
machine learning stuff,

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:16.470
than your Hive analytics,
than-- what is it--

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.420
business reporting--

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:19.950
also quite big.

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.410
But as you guys
probably already know,

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:26.310
machine learning consumes
quite some power, especially

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:27.965
compute power.

00:19:27.965 --> 00:19:30.204
And for that, you can
create a different workload.

00:19:30.204 --> 00:19:31.620
You can create a
different cluster

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:36.180
with a different configuration,
with lots of CPUs, GPUs, VPUs,

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:40.650
maybe, and low on memory maybe.

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:43.290
So this is also, note,
something to think about when

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.870
you start moving forward.

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:51.750
And of course, we can kill
the surface quite fast.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:57.280
So if you use it, then you say
OK, drop it and throw it away.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:00.420
But there's something called
a semi-long-lived cluster.

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:03.540
And actually, that's
very interesting.

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:05.900
So consider this
following situation--

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:07.310
I have to keep
track of the time.

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:08.684
Consider the
following situation,

00:20:08.684 --> 00:20:10.040
that you have your cluster--

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:15.210
it's production, it's
version 1, whatever--

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:19.566
but Hadoop has a new release
coming up which is 10x faster,

00:20:19.566 --> 00:20:21.540
or maybe has-- the
whole complexity

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:23.400
that you're handling
in pipelines,

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:24.630
reduces it to one command.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:30.760
The nice part is you can start
doing load balancing between--

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:33.930
and canary testing-- for those
who know that term, that's

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:35.850
about trying the
latest, greatest,

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:39.910
but not breaking your
whole production.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:43.440
So you can create clusters
with the newest version

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:46.212
and make it part of
your production process.

00:20:46.212 --> 00:20:47.670
You can even start
testing, will it

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:49.086
work for me, will
it help me, will

00:20:49.086 --> 00:20:51.840
it create value in the end.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:53.580
So we have many--

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:57.210
so you can start creating
clusters in a group.

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:59.760
And that acts as one.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.724
And just test it with your
data, see if it works.

00:21:02.724 --> 00:21:04.140
And at some moment,
you could say,

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:07.200
I'm upgrading all my
clusters to that version.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:11.310
That's actually, like,
four lines or four clicks.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:15.790
That's really impressive
if you see that in action.

