00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:59.550
is that in the--

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:00.180
was it here?

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:01.890
This one?

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:04.120
If I refresh this
one, I can see that--

00:28:11.305 --> 00:28:12.630
it's needing some time here.

00:28:16.518 --> 00:28:19.100
OK, it will be
there in a moment.

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:21.610
But then, you can see
the default flow of all

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:23.110
the processes that are running.

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:24.940
This is still native Hadoop.

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:27.290
You're not doing
something special here.

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:30.790
So another way to run jobs--

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:35.960
because the thing is that if I--

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:37.270
this one is finished.

00:28:37.270 --> 00:28:40.360
Unfortunately, I can Control-C
it, and then I'm lost, right?

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:44.200
Then I have to
restart the job again.

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:47.320
But what we can
do here, also, is

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:52.120
I can fire up Cloud
Dataproc project--

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:54.670
sorry, a job-- on my cluster.

00:28:58.452 --> 00:28:59.910
And this might be
a long statement.

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:06.070
But what it's doing,
it's firing off a job.

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:06.570
Sorry.

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:14.540
It's firing off-- so what we
do is Google Cloud Dataproc job

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:17.770
submit Hadoop, and
then, the same map

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:20.220
reduce example that you
saw a moment ago, only

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:22.160
I didn't provide it parameters.

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.800
The reason why I did that
is to see this error log,

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:25.980
because I did it on purpose.

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:27.860
It's not the demo
error, by the way.

00:29:27.860 --> 00:29:30.850
Hopefully nothing will
be coming up also.

00:29:30.850 --> 00:29:33.480
But what you see
now is that, OK,

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.450
I have an error, so
I have to fix it.

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:40.520
So the next step is
to fix this statement.

00:29:40.520 --> 00:29:43.850
And what we're going to
do is we're going to say--

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:55.150
sorry, we're going
to say teragen,

00:29:55.150 --> 00:29:57.820
like we just did a moment ago.

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:04.840
And then I can say it's on
the Google Cloud Storage Next

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:09.175
Amsterdam bucket, and
create the teragen-temp2.

00:30:14.125 --> 00:30:17.095
Oh, did I miss something?

00:30:24.025 --> 00:30:25.520
Yep.

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:29.180
So now it's submitting a job,
and that's actually very cool.

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:31.880
Submitting a job in
Google Dataproc is fun.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:33.620
Because everything
that everybody

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:35.540
does with your system,
you can record it.

00:30:35.540 --> 00:30:37.460
It will be recorded actually.

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:42.140
And what I can do now
is, while it's running,

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:44.510
I can go to the Cloud console.

00:30:44.510 --> 00:30:49.260
And if I look at my clusters, I
see that jobs are being fired.

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:53.710
So I had two wrong
jobs apparently.

00:30:53.710 --> 00:30:54.460
I can see the log.

00:30:54.460 --> 00:30:56.120
And one is running
at this moment.

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:05.630
So another thing to do
is-- if I say Control-C,

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:08.260
I'm not losing the job.

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:14.620
Because what I can do is I
can say gcloud dataproc jobs

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:22.410
await, and then I take
the job ID, Control-C it.

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:31.930
And what it will do is
reconnect to the stream that is

00:31:31.930 --> 00:31:35.720
being generated by the output.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:37.400
And in the end, I'm finished.

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:39.470
So that's something
that you can get.

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:41.470
You can start a job, close
your laptop, go home,

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:45.970
and it will still be executing
anyhow in the background.

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:49.494
So another thing that I
would like to show you--

00:31:49.494 --> 00:31:50.410
and I still have time?

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:50.910
Yep.

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:53.810
Nice-- is a thing
called a Stackdriver.

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:57.940
So Stackdriver is like our
monitoring tools over Cloud

00:31:57.940 --> 00:32:00.440
environments, if you want to.

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:04.090
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to create a group here,

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:06.040
call it rokesh-demo-live.

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:14.470
And I'm going to add a tag.

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:21.160
And in one of my first
statements that's here,

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:23.820
I usually tag this.

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:25.660
So the tag is actually
quite interesting.

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:29.140
Because now you can combine
several clusters together

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:33.670
and have a dashboard on
all the clusters in one

00:32:33.670 --> 00:32:36.220
go, although they're totally
independent, totally not

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:37.510
related maybe, even.

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:42.987
But you can see the complete
performance of your clusters

00:32:42.987 --> 00:32:43.820
that you're running.

00:32:46.688 --> 00:32:53.670
Persist, add criteria-- bang.

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:54.516
Save group.

00:32:54.516 --> 00:32:57.710
Oh, this one can go.

00:32:57.710 --> 00:32:58.337
Save group.

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:03.940
So in Stackdriver, the
very interesting part

00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:12.024
is, within a Stackdriver group,
you can create dashboards.

00:33:12.024 --> 00:33:14.190
And the dashboard is actually
quite straightforward.

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:19.210
Because you can
say, for example--

00:33:19.210 --> 00:33:21.750
let's see-- the inbound traffic.

00:33:25.229 --> 00:33:26.223
Where is it?

00:33:30.199 --> 00:33:31.193
It's a bit slow.

00:33:31.193 --> 00:33:32.187
I don't know why.

00:33:35.666 --> 00:33:41.140
GCP-- yep.

00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:43.780
So we have the network
inbound, we make a stack area,

00:33:43.780 --> 00:33:48.905
and we filter on
group where the group

00:33:48.905 --> 00:33:52.570
value is equal to my demo.

00:33:52.570 --> 00:33:54.640
And then I say Save.

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:56.900
And what I can do
is, now, on the fly,

00:33:56.900 --> 00:34:00.920
add charts on the
cluster itself.

00:34:00.920 --> 00:34:03.997
This is something that is very
powerful which allows you to,

00:34:03.997 --> 00:34:05.830
indeed, have an overview
of all your classes

00:34:05.830 --> 00:34:08.149
running at the same time.

00:34:08.149 --> 00:34:09.940
Of course, we have
something similar, also,

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:12.400
in the cluster
environment where,

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:18.710
if you click on a cluster, you
can see the CPU utilization.

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:22.120
These are apparently the jobs
that I'd tried a moment ago.

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:24.456
Network bytes,
capacity, et cetera,

00:34:24.456 --> 00:34:25.830
it's everything
that you can see.

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:29.906
But the biggest difference
with Stackdriver

00:34:29.906 --> 00:34:31.280
is that you can
see it in one go.

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:35.290
You can make a dashboard out
of it and show it everywhere.

00:34:35.290 --> 00:34:38.920
So I think it's time to
do something really beefy.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:40.570
So what I did,
before this demo, I

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:45.070
created, with TeraGen, 100
gigabytes of sorting data.

00:34:45.070 --> 00:34:47.717
And I would like to have my
Hadoop cluster work on it.

00:34:52.390 --> 00:34:54.848
So let's go back to this one.

00:34:54.848 --> 00:34:55.889
So this is the statement.

00:34:55.889 --> 00:34:57.122
It's the same thing.

00:34:57.122 --> 00:34:58.955
The only things that
we're doing differently

00:34:58.955 --> 00:35:00.890
is, now, we're doing TeraSort.

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:02.275
So that's the sorting algorithm.

00:35:02.275 --> 00:35:03.310
It's for benchmarking.

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:05.740
So we can see what the
performance is of that.

00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:08.500
And I will do it on
100 gigabytes of data.

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:15.390
So I submit the job.

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:20.400
And what you see now is that
it's 100 gigabytes of data.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:22.430
It's not very easy to do that.

00:35:22.430 --> 00:35:25.570
It's going to cost some time.

00:35:25.570 --> 00:35:29.310
So what I'm waiting for now is
to see the percentages go up.

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:35.940
And if we see it in a bit--
so it's going to be, like,

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:40.680
every 20 seconds, it's
going to do 1%, which

00:35:40.680 --> 00:35:43.086
is not that fast, right?

00:35:43.086 --> 00:35:44.460
And I already
created my cluster.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:46.530
So what to do now?

00:35:46.530 --> 00:35:51.420
So what you can do is--

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:58.170
so while this one is doing, say,
about 20 seconds, more or less.

00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:01.050
What we can do now is we
can go back to the cluster,

00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:02.310
go into the configuration.

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:05.310
And this is the easiest
thing I've ever seen.

00:36:05.310 --> 00:36:10.486
I say Edit, let's say, 40.

00:36:10.486 --> 00:36:13.080
I want to have 40 worker nodes.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:18.450
So what happens now is
the request is submitted.

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:19.304
It's not canceled.

00:36:19.304 --> 00:36:20.970
Yesterday I had a
cancellation because I

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:22.470
didn't have enough quota.

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:28.380
But now my cluster is creating
40 VM instances immediately.

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:32.440
In a moment, they are
available and managed.

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:36.130
The calculation that is
happening will start using it.

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:38.190
So it's spanning
from five workers

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:40.680
immediately to 40 workers.

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:42.930
And hopefully, the
result in a bit

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:48.210
is that you will see that this
job will go faster and faster.

00:36:48.210 --> 00:36:52.050
I'll leave it open
in the background.

00:36:52.050 --> 00:36:57.930
So yeah, another thing that
is very interesting to show

00:36:57.930 --> 00:37:04.750
in Stackdriver itself is,
if you look at something

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:08.770
called fork rate and memory.

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:13.140
And this is actually-- oh,
let me stick to memory only--

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:14.490
memory use.

00:37:14.490 --> 00:37:19.285
So line chart, filter, group.

00:37:22.231 --> 00:37:24.690
Save.

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:27.060
So the interesting part is--

00:37:27.060 --> 00:37:29.700
and if I zoom in a bit--

00:37:29.700 --> 00:37:33.010
here, you can now see which VM--

00:37:33.010 --> 00:37:36.560
sorry, it's invisible.

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:39.570
You can see which VM is
taking lots of memory,

00:37:39.570 --> 00:37:41.650
is taking up all the memory.

00:37:41.650 --> 00:37:44.090
And what you'll see at a
moment is, if all the clusters

00:37:44.090 --> 00:37:45.880
come together and the 40--

00:37:45.880 --> 00:37:49.040
sorry, the 40 VMs
are available--

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:52.010
now you see them
all spiking also.

00:37:52.010 --> 00:37:53.689
So actually, you can
see where there's

00:37:53.689 --> 00:37:54.980
a bottleneck in your framework.

00:37:54.980 --> 00:37:57.500
And you can say, OK, it might
be time to add some memory

00:37:57.500 --> 00:38:00.260
or accept compute power.

00:38:00.260 --> 00:38:03.720
So that's actually
quite impressive also.

00:38:06.359 --> 00:38:07.900
Oh yeah, another
thing that I totally

00:38:07.900 --> 00:38:11.630
forget, but is very
important-- so I

00:38:11.630 --> 00:38:16.280
created a cluster which was in
a different preview version.

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:20.030
So what I can do now,
here, is I can go to 3.

00:38:20.030 --> 00:38:21.960
That's my third
cluster that I created.

00:38:21.960 --> 00:38:24.260
And if the network is working--

00:38:24.260 --> 00:38:26.170
yes, it is.

00:38:26.170 --> 00:38:29.390
It's actually quite slow--

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:30.920
I'll end up in
that cluster, which

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:32.772
is apparently also running.

00:38:32.772 --> 00:38:34.051
Oh, sorry.

00:38:34.051 --> 00:38:34.550
About?

00:38:39.580 --> 00:38:41.720
You can imagine that,
yesterday, the demo was fast.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:47.350
But if it's there--

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:49.810
what I'm showing here
is that I'm actually

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:53.185
using another version
of Hadoop, which

00:38:53.185 --> 00:38:57.670
is version 2.8's snapshot, which
is made available by Google

00:38:57.670 --> 00:38:58.420
for you.

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:01.210
And we're taking the
latest snapshot each day.

00:39:01.210 --> 00:39:04.940
So that allows you to start
doing the canary testing part.

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:10.300
So if all the things are done,
and you say, OK, it's fine.

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:11.830
My job is going faster now.

00:39:11.830 --> 00:39:15.870
Instead of just
every 20 seconds,

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:18.293
it's now every two seconds,
or something like that.

00:39:18.293 --> 00:39:20.860
You can say, OK, fine,
kill my clusters.

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:24.600
So killing of cluster or
removing a cluster can be done.

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:28.930
And something I would
like, definitely,

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:33.130
to share with your guys is
that if you have a cluster--

00:39:33.130 --> 00:39:37.780
or, sorry, if you
say create cluster,

00:39:37.780 --> 00:39:41.620
there is always a REST
interface available for that.

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:42.740
Why is this important?

00:39:42.740 --> 00:39:47.830
Because people found out that
we need orchestration tools

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:49.797
like Airflow and
Luigi to make sure

00:39:49.797 --> 00:39:51.880
that they can interact
with these kind of clusters

00:39:51.880 --> 00:39:54.080
and manage it.

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:55.720
So for all the
actions that you've

00:39:55.720 --> 00:40:01.350
seen that I've been doing
today, you can find another--

00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:04.330
sorry, the REST version of that.

00:40:04.330 --> 00:40:10.480
Going forward, deleting a
cluster is like selecting it,

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:13.330
throwing it away--
bang, that's it.

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:16.130
And that's also
everything that I would

00:40:16.130 --> 00:40:19.170
like to show in this demo.

00:40:19.170 --> 00:40:21.420
I see we have two minutes.

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:23.500
So managing
clusters, what we did

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:27.430
is we showed that you can
go through raw clients

00:40:27.430 --> 00:40:31.980
or through the user
interface of Cloud Dataproc,

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:34.940
create clusters,
jobs, and operations.

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:39.370
We have low-level
APIs versus jobs APIs.

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:42.580
All of them, we don't want
to frustrate you, right?

00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:46.390
So if you're used to Hadoop
jobs, go ahead, make a date,

00:40:46.390 --> 00:40:48.310
start using Hadoop.

00:40:48.310 --> 00:40:50.860
But if you want to use a bit
more sophisticated approach

00:40:50.860 --> 00:40:53.510
where jobs are not killed
because you closed your laptop

00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:56.380
or because there was
a network outage, then

00:40:56.380 --> 00:40:59.100
these are the other
ways to go forward too.

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:00.850
Everything is REST-based,
so all the calls

00:41:00.850 --> 00:41:02.516
that are made through
the gcloud command

00:41:02.516 --> 00:41:05.050
can be done through REST.

00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:07.600
Updating, downscaling,
and upscaling

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:10.630
can be done through the Edit
button, also through REST

00:41:10.630 --> 00:41:12.190
if you want to.

00:41:12.190 --> 00:41:15.190
Logging and monitoring
is part of the platform.

00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:17.890
And even if you have multiple
clusters and a big environment,

00:41:17.890 --> 00:41:21.910
which is going to happen
anyhow, you can do it.

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:24.430
So putting it all together,
reference architecture

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:27.632
for burst loads is used
in internet gateway.

00:41:27.632 --> 00:41:29.590
And then, we're going to
fire off in the cloud.

00:41:29.590 --> 00:41:32.010
We use it, and we throw it away.

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:33.960
And a hybrid
approach is possible,

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:37.510
where you use a VPN gateway
to talk, and bring data

00:41:37.510 --> 00:41:40.300
back and forth, and do your
job wherever you want to,

00:41:40.300 --> 00:41:43.570
especially if the data
is not necessarily

00:41:43.570 --> 00:41:46.120
allowed to be in the cloud.

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:51.340
And you can go full-cloud
native, which, in the long run,

00:41:51.340 --> 00:41:55.630
will help you with canary
testing, with upgrading.

00:41:55.630 --> 00:41:58.510
I would never accept
an argument that says,

00:41:58.510 --> 00:42:01.250
you know, it's been
running for two years.

00:42:01.250 --> 00:42:02.500
Let's keep it.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:05.620
Let's not touch it.

00:42:05.620 --> 00:42:07.870
In this case, you can start
canary testing immediately

00:42:07.870 --> 00:42:09.760
and say, OK, part
of my workload is

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:12.410
going to go to that
new Hadoop cluster.

00:42:12.410 --> 00:42:16.210
And we'll see if there's a
performance gain in that.

00:42:16.210 --> 00:42:19.030
So if you want to get
started with Cloud Dataproc,

00:42:19.030 --> 00:42:20.860
these are the links.

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:23.090
Tutorials are available.

00:42:23.090 --> 00:42:26.110
This code sample can
be made available.

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:30.900
So find me if you want to dive
into these kinds of contexts.

00:42:30.900 --> 00:42:35.226
And yeah, for documentation,
you can find quite a bit.

00:42:35.226 --> 00:42:36.850
I did my first
presentation on Dataproc

00:42:36.850 --> 00:42:38.050
one and a half years ago.

00:42:38.050 --> 00:42:40.060
This is more or less
the same product

00:42:40.060 --> 00:42:42.560
with lots of extra
features on top of it.

00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:45.370
And since then-- so
since the launch,

00:42:45.370 --> 00:42:47.280
like, one and a half
years ago-- there's

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:50.720
lots of content, also, to
be found on Stack Overflow.

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:53.675
Follow the discussions,
and find us in support.

00:42:53.675 --> 00:42:55.300
And for the rest,
I'd like to thank you

00:42:55.300 --> 00:42:56.837
for your time and patience.

00:42:56.837 --> 00:42:57.670
Thank you very much.

00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:01.020
[APPLAUSE]

